Inside, the reader is a woman named Madame Celeste, although a framed cosmetology license on the wall says Debra M. Phillips.

Cage notices this immediately.

Doesn’t mention it.

Yet.

Celeste gestures toward the chair.

“Sit.”

Cage sits.

Little Nick sits on the table.

Celeste looks at Little Nick.

Long pause.

“Is that your son?”

Cage: “Complicated.”

Little Nick: “Operationally, no.”

Celeste chooses not to pursue this, which Cage immediately respects.

She takes his hand.

Looks at the palm.

Her expression changes.

Not dramatically.

Just enough.

Cage notices.

“What.”

Celeste: “Nothing.”

“You saw something.”

“I see many things.”

“What did you see?”

Jessica from the corner:

“Nick, she’s had your hand for nine seconds.”

Cage, without looking away:

“NINE SECONDS IS ENOUGH TO IDENTIFY A FRACTURE.”

Celeste studies his palm again.

Then she says:

“You’ve lived many lives.”

Cage goes absolutely still.

Little Nick whispers:

“Ohhhh, fuck.”

Jessica: “That’s literally what palm readers say to everyone.”

Cage turns his head slowly.

“Jessica.”

“What?”

“She doesn’t know about Moon Cop.”

Celeste: “What’s Moon Cop?”

Cage turns back.

“Good.”

Jessica: “WHAT DOES THAT PROVE?”

“Compartmentalization.”

